# Copy to /home/andrew/pump_poller/poller.env and edit. Loaded by
# pump-poller.service (EnvironmentFile). Restart with:
#   sudo systemctl restart pump-poller
# KEY=value, no quotes, no spaces around '='.

# ── Modbus target ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On Andrew's desk (remote): leave as the DuckDNS name.
# In the pump shed on the modem LAN: use the CIM500's static lease so the
# 1-second polling is local and free (no cellular data, immune to DuckDNS).
#GRUNDFOS_HOST=192.168.1.2
#GRUNDFOS_PORT=502

# ── DuckDNS guard (on-site self-heal) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Telstra reissues the SIM's public IP from time to time and the USR modem's
# DDNS client has failed to push it (23 Aug 2026: 14 h outage). With these set,
# the poller asks the modem for its real wan_4g IP every 5 min over the LAN,
# publishes a heartbeat to Supabase (pump_router_status - used by the GitHub
# poll workflow's self-heal), and re-points DuckDNS itself if DUCKDNS_TOKEN
# is given. Only meaningful when the Pi is behind the modem.
#ROUTER_URL=http://192.168.1.1
#ROUTER_USER=root
#ROUTER_PASSWORD=the-modem-luci-password
#ROUTER_WAN_IFACE=wan_4g
# Alternative to the ubus RPC above: any shell command that prints the WAN IP,
# e.g. over SSH with a key installed on the modem:
#ROUTER_IP_CMD=ssh -o BatchMode=yes root@192.168.1.1 "ifstatus wan_4g | jsonfilter -e '@[\"ipv4-address\"][0].address'"
#DUCKDNS_TOKEN=your-duckdns-token
#DUCKDNS_DOMAIN=bidgee-pumps
#DDNS_CHECK_S=300
